  "query_preview": "..\n\nA moist airmass is in place over Lower Michigan, as both 12z DTX/APX \nRAOBS came in with a 5 C dew pt at 700 MB. Waves of isentropic \nascent have been supportive of widespread showers. Fortunately, \ninstability is limited, and rainfall amounts have been manageable, \ngenerally in the range of a quarter of an inch (south) to one inch \n(north). \n\nAn upper level ridge/heat dome over the central Plains will drift \nnortheast, reaching southern Illinois by Monday evening. The \nextension of the ridge axis over the northern Mississippi River \nValley will subsequently move overhead into the central Great Lakes \non Monday as well. High temperatures will be dependent on how well \nthe warm front and tonight's expected showers and thunderstorms \nclear southeast Michigan Monday morning. The north half of the CWA \nis favored location, as local probabilistic guidance suggests pops \naoa 70 percent north of M-46. \n\nWith 850 MB temps progged to rise into the 20-21 C range Monday-\nWednesday, max temp potential is certainly aoa 90. However, much \nlike today, any thick cloud cover and shower/thunder activity during \nthe day could short-circuit max temps. In addition, the outgoing NBM \nsurface dew pts appear to be too high (widespread lower 70s), so \nadvertised heat indices appear too high as well. \n\nThe upper-level ridge is already flattened on Tuesday over Lower \nMichigan, with westerly flow in place as 500 MB low tracks through \nnorthern Ontario. At least moderate instability building up in a \nweakly capped environment suggests that there will be some diurnal \nactivity, especially with any enhanced low level convergence/lake \nbreeze/marine influence. Sufficient bulk shear to support a chance \nof severe storms, if in fact storms do go up. \n\nA slight drying out of the deeper moisture in place as we head into \nWednesday could support the warmest maxes of the week, per the NBM. \nStill, confidence is not high, as height falls arrive by Wednesday \nevening/night and a potentially active baroclinic/frontal zone \ndevelops over southern Lower Michigan into Thursday.",
